@page "/Home"

<MudText Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-4" Typo="Typo.h2">OSDC Well</MudText>
<MudDivider Class="mb-4" />
<MudText Class="mx-6 px-6" Typo="Typo.body1">
    This microservice manages Well records: their identity assignments, feature assignments, location within a
    Cluster and Slot, and portable versioned backups. It persists to SQLite, enforces optimistic concurrency on
    every mutation, and exposes the same operations through a REST API, an MCP server, and a Blazor web
    application, so a well record created or edited in one interface is immediately visible through the others.
</MudText>

<MudGrid Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-2">
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">What is a Well</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A <b>Well</b> is the administrative record for one well: a name, a description, server-owned
                creation and modification timestamps, an optional location (<code>ClusterID</code> and
                <code>SlotID</code>), and the identity and feature assignments that describe it. It is the anchor
                other drilling data — trajectories, survey runs, wellbores — refers back to, without itself carrying
                trajectory geometry or saying how many subsurface targets it reaches.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Well UUIDs are caller-generated so that consuming systems can mint and reference an identifier before
                the record exists on the server, which matters when a well is planned before it is spudded.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Wells versus wellbores</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A Well is not the same thing as a <b>WellBore</b>. One Well record covers the entire well; a
                WellBore is one physical drilled path belonging to it, with its own geometry, survey stations, and
                casing. A well is rarely aimed at a single point — it is more often planned against a
                <b>target group</b>, several subsurface targets it needs to reach. Targets in the group can be
                reached <i>in series</i>, drilled through one after another along the same bore, or split across
                separate branches, as in a sidetracked or multilateral well where each lateral departs from the
                original hole to reach a different target.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                This is why the two are different microservices: <b>Well</b> owns the location, identity, and
                feature record for the well as a whole, while the separate <b>WellBore</b> service owns the
                individual drilled-path definitions — the original hole and any sidetracks — each linking back to
                its Well through an optional <code>WellID</code>.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Single wells and wells in a cluster</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A well can stand alone, with no <code>ClusterID</code>, or it can be located at a <b>Slot</b> within
                a <b>Cluster</b> — a template, platform, or pad shared by several wells. The <code>IsSingleWell</code>
                flag on the Cluster record distinguishes a genuine multi-slot cluster from a "cluster" that exists
                only to carry the location of one standalone well, so both cases share the same location model.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                <code>ClusterID</code> and <code>SlotID</code> are external references owned by the Cluster
                microservice: Well validates their shape and the Slot-requires-Cluster rule locally, and offers
                read-only <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/Well">validation and audit</MudLink> against the live Cluster
                service, but never blocks a Well write on that other service being reachable.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Identities and features</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Every well can carry any number of user-defined <b>identities</b> — symbolic references such as a
                UWI, an operator well name, or a regulatory ID — each with a well-specific value, and any number of
                <b>feature</b> assignments drawn from user-defined categories and options, optionally exclusive
                within a category and optionally time-bounded.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                The identity and feature <i>catalogs</i> are managed on the
                <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/WellIdentities">Well Identities</MudLink> and
                <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/WellFeatures">Well Features</MudLink> pages, while a specific well's
                assignments are added, replaced, or removed one at a time from the <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/Well">Well</MudLink>
                page — a definition or option cannot be deleted while a well still references it.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Contextual data and calculators</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The web application's <b>Contextual Data</b> menu gives read access to the records a well's location relies
        on: <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/Cluster">Cluster</MudLink>, <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/Field">Field</MudLink>,
        and <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/Rig">Rig</MudLink> — each backed by its own microservice and shown here
        through reusable Razor components, so a well can be understood in its field and drilling-program context
        without leaving this application.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <b>Calculators</b> menu exposes the same synchronous, stateless evaluations used elsewhere in OSDC —
        <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/FieldCartographicConverter">Cartographic Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/EarthVerticalDatumCalculation">Vertical Datum Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/EarthGravityCalculation">Earth Gravity Evaluation</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/EarthMagneticFieldCalculation">Earth Magnetic Field Evaluation</MudLink> —
        for one-off conversions and lookups while working a well, without switching to another site.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Backup and restore</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Backups are logical, portable JSON, not a raw database copy. From the
        <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/WellBackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink> page, export <b>all</b> wells or
        an ordered <b>selection</b> into one versioned document that carries the complete wells together with only
        the identity and feature catalog entries they actually reference — enough to reproduce the wells on another
        installation without pulling in unrelated catalog clutter.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Restoring the same document back offers a <b>conflict policy</b> — fail if any well UUID already exists, or
        replace matching wells while creating the rest — and a <b>catalog policy</b> — map onto existing local
        identity/feature definitions by UUID or name, or create the missing ones locally. Validation, catalog
        mapping, and every well write happen inside one SQLite transaction, so a restore of many wells either
        commits completely or leaves the database untouched.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Data integrity and monitoring</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Well updates, deletes, and individual identity/feature assignment changes use <b>optimistic concurrency</b>:
        every mutation must echo back the <code>LastModificationDate</code> from the caller's latest read, and a
        stale request is rejected rather than silently overwriting a concurrent change. Deleting a catalog
        definition or option still in use by a well is likewise rejected instead of cascading into well data.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Because <code>ClusterID</code> and <code>SlotID</code> point at another service, Well also offers read-only
        <b>external-reference validation</b> for one well and a bounded <b>audit</b> across all or selected wells,
        each reference reported as <code>Valid</code>, <code>Invalid</code>, or <code>Unavailable</code> so a
        dependency outage is never confused with a genuinely broken reference. The
        <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/StatisticsWell">Usage Statistics</MudLink> page reports per-endpoint request
        counts for this service replica.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Using this web application</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Open <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/Well">Well</MudLink> to browse, search, create, edit, and remove wells and
        their identity and feature assignments. Manage the shared catalogs on
        <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/WellIdentities">Well Identities</MudLink> and
        <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/WellFeatures">Well Features</MudLink>, and move data between installations on
        <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/WellBackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink>.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <b>Survey Display</b> menu shows <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/WellTrajectories">Well Trajectories</MudLink>
        and <MudLink Href="/Well/webapp/WellSurveyRuns">Well Survey Runs</MudLink> with Rig and mean-sea-level
        depth-reference integration, and the <b>Contextual Data</b> and <b>Calculators</b> menus give access to the
        related records and evaluations described above. All numeric fields follow the unit system selected for the
        page through the OSDC unit-reference system.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4 mb-6" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Access methods</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
        The same well data is reachable through several interfaces built on the same underlying store, so it can be
        managed interactively, scripted, embedded in other Blazor applications, or wired into AI agents.
    </MudText>
    <MudSimpleTable>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Web page</b></td>
                <td>
                    This site — a server-side Blazor application (this page) for browsing, editing, backing up, and
                    restoring wells, inspecting contextual data, running calculators, and reviewing usage statistics.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>REST API</b></td>
                <td>
                    JSON endpoints under <code>/Well/api</code> for well CRUD, bounded search, identity and feature
                    catalog management, targeted assignment mutations, external-reference validation and audit, and
                    batch export/restore, documented and explorable through the Swagger UI at
                    <code>/Well/api/swagger</code>. Suited to scripts, backend integration, and any HTTP client.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>MCP</b></td>
                <td>
                    A Model Context Protocol server exposed as Streamable HTTP at <code>/Well/api/mcp</code> (and
                    WebSocket at <code>/Well/api/mcp/ws</code>), publishing 36 tools — every non-statistics REST
                    operation plus connectivity <code>ping</code> — with strict, closed input/output JSON Schemas, so
                    AI agents and MCP-aware tooling can query, create, update, and back up wells directly.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Reusable web pages</b></td>
                <td>
                    The Razor components behind the Well, catalog, backup/restore, and survey-display pages are
                    published as the <code>OSDC.Drilling.Well.WebPages</code> NuGet package, so other Blazor
                    applications — such as the Cluster web app — can embed the same unit-aware controls instead of
                    re-implementing this UI.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
</MudPaper>
